{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"565fc40ea456427614b26c64de085a1e64dcdf6465a669ab2993ebda3c8e2d6c","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_cc3c022138da397946b014749b055370cf9bb28ee1bb8d340fd106b93cc6a1c2","overall_assessment":"The paper introduces a Fast Chirplet Transform (FCT) as a bio-inspired representation to pretrain CNNs for audio classification. The significance is primarily demonstrated through computational efficiency (reduced training epochs) and modest accuracy gains on small-scale datasets (BIRD10 and TIMIT vowels). The work offers a unique experimental approach by integrating signal processing priors into deep learning, but the impact is currently limited by the small size of the validation datasets and the lack of comparison against broader state-of-the-art baselines.","paper_id":"H1Fk2Iqex","paper_identity_sha256":"43dbcbd0eb9b6bbd5c3ac2f26b04fed5241aaf2d08a7c3f05013fa5e6584ff73","prompt_identity_sha256":"d09b73fd8dea3d61f756d5968fae908af1be7a27fa3cb786fc054f9c750ac212","raw_response_sha256":"cdcd99944925d4bd50cdce268b49f09c8ef3d39ae44390ff78eb6f32d1ccbbce","request_identity_sha256":"28dfff55d2ea79f812ab49e4eac4b3b68047d0e6fa20c7e219fdbad41b6f4ed7","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"c19d61d08c61d0a7a677e36b5113d456e1856d4b5c07aac3cc60dd29ea83d34b","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-004","SRC-024"],"review_text":"The work addresses the challenge of audio representation for machine listening, particularly in scenarios with limited data or low signal-to-noise ratios. By proposing a Chirplet-based transform that subsumes Fourier and wavelet analysis, the paper introduces a representation grounded in neurobiological auditory processing. This approach has the potential to influence research at the intersection of signal processing and deep learning, offering a structured alternative to purely data-driven feature learning. The proposed method could serve as a useful prior for other time-frequency analysis tasks, though its broader applicability beyond bioacoustics and speech is currently speculative.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-BRD"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-013","SRC-016","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The paper demonstrates domain-specific impact in bioacoustics (bird and whale classification) and speech (vowel recognition). The FCT is validated on the BIRD10 subset of the LifeClef challenge and the TIMIT vowel subset. The method shows utility in these specialized fields by leveraging the frequency modulation characteristics inherent in animal calls and speech. However, the domain impact is constrained by the small scale of the datasets used for validation (e.g., 15 minutes of bird recordings, 192 test utterances for vowels), which limits the generalizability of the domain-specific benefits to larger, more complex real-world applications.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-DOM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-019","SRC-021","SRC-023"],"review_text":"The paper reports improvements over baselines (raw audio and Mel spectrograms) in terms of training efficiency and accuracy. Specifically, FCT pretraining reduces training duration by 28% for birds and 26% for vowels, with relative accuracy gains of 7.8% (MAP) and 2.3% (Accuracy), respectively. While these gains are consistent within the reported experiments, the baselines are relatively simple (raw audio, Mel) and do not represent the current state-of-the-art in audio classification. Furthermore, the authors explicitly note that the vowel experiment does not allow for comparison with other papers due to the specific setup, and the bird dataset is very small (3 species). Therefore, the magnitude of improvement relative to the broader state of the art is not fully established.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-SOT"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-003","SRC-024"],"review_text":"The paper does not explicitly discuss the societal, environmental, or ethical implications of the proposed method. While the application to environmental monitoring (e.g., Orca recordings) suggests potential benefits for conservation, the paper focuses primarily on the technical performance of the FCT and its integration with CNNs. There is no discussion of who benefits from this technology, potential risks of misclassification in sensitive ecological contexts, or the environmental impact of the computational resources required. The real-world impact is implied through the domain application but not critically evaluated in terms of societal ramifications.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-IMP"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"d1f3f7b174967545c34e54d9135f47e0283704559367eafe0ff9e7cb0fdc8da2","paper_id":"H1Fk2Iqex","paper_ordinal":86,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"cdcd99944925d4bd50cdce268b49f09c8ef3d39ae44390ff78eb6f32d1ccbbce","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"H1Fk2Iqex","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"47702fb6ea41e2ceffe6db848695774a44b4b61e9cef7dbf26e74e91bc5d200c","request_identity_sha256":"28dfff55d2ea79f812ab49e4eac4b3b68047d0e6fa20c7e219fdbad41b6f4ed7","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":911,"prompt_tokens":14099,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":15010},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"28dfff55d2ea79f812ab49e4eac4b3b68047d0e6fa20c7e219fdbad41b6f4ed7","role":"SIGN","role_ordinal":2,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}